<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  What the hardened runtime is asked to allow, and nothing more.

  Every entry here is a hole in a wall macOS puts up by default, so the list is
  short on purpose: an entitlement granted "just in case" is one nobody can
  later prove is unnecessary.

  NOT present, deliberately:

  - `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`. This app SPAWNS `codex exec`, and a
    sandboxed process cannot execute an arbitrary binary outside its container.
    Sandboxing would not weaken delegation, it would delete it. Developer ID
    distribution does not require the sandbox; the App Store does, which is one
    of several reasons this app is not headed there.
  - `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation`. Only needed to load
    native code signed by somebody else, and there are no runtime native
    dependencies — `@napi-rs/canvas` is a devDependency used by the icon script,
    which never ships. If a native module is added later and refuses to load,
    that is the moment to reconsider, with a reason.
  - `com.apple.security.network.client`. Meaningful only under the sandbox.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <!--
      V8 compiles JavaScript at run time. Without these two, Electron does not
      start under the hardened runtime at all — the failure is a crash on
      launch, which looks nothing like a signing problem and is one.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      She listens. This is the entitlement, and `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription`
      in Info.plist is the sentence the user reads when macOS asks: both are
      required, and missing either produces a permission dialog that never
      appears and a microphone that never opens.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
